{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>Behold, this day they have offered</b> — What does this mean? Rather, Moses said to them: Perhaps you sprinkled its blood while אוננים (onenim, mourners before burial of the deceased), so that the onen who performed the service profaned it? Aaron said to him: Did they offer it, seeing as they are laymen? I brought it near, for I am the High Priest and an onen may offer. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 10:19). And so it is in the Gemara in chapter טבול יום (Zevaḥim 101a): 'But perhaps you offered it and disqualified it?' And I am astonished, for all the offerings were made before the period of mourning, as it is written: 'And he came down from making the sin-offering and the burnt-offering and the peace-offerings' (Leviticus 9:22), and afterward they entered the Tent of Meeting and prayed, and after that the fire came down upon the offerings, and then Nadav and Avihu offered this incense. And perhaps Moses did not see their actions, and suspected that perhaps they found the blood of this goat that had not yet been sprinkled and sprinkled it. And Aaron said to him: Was not the sprinkling mine, and when it was sprinkled from my hand it was sprinkled and was not disqualified by virtue of mourning. And this was a give-and-take of halakhah, but nothing at all was done by them in a state of mourning."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The phrase משא ומתן של הלכה (literally 'lifting and carrying of law') refers to Talmudic dialectical argumentation; rendered as 'give-and-take of halakhah.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:52:22.641093651Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}